Dear Laura

Whether you use a two baselines or a two baselines full model it depends if you consider a discrimination factor for carbon. Looking at your HPN plot of Parasesarma bidens, it seems that you are missing at least one baseline. If your crab is feeding on brown leaves, then the TDF for carbon would be roughly 10 permil, and if they are feeding on MPB then the TDF still is too high.

Remember that if you use a two baselines model, the consumer should be in-between the two sources, otherwise the model will not calculate anything useful.

I suggest using only a one baseline model, unless you know for sure which is the energetic pathway that is feeding your consumers.

It seems also that ggplot2 changed one of their functions a few months ago, so now it is a "bug" that the colours are not displaying well. Apologies for this.

Regarding the issue for checking the model, this is just to check if a normal distribution is correct for your consumer (you only has 3 observations!) and your baselines (for MPB you only have 2 data!). So it is unlikely that the model will perform well.

Cheers

Claudio

